[[Lake Haro]] is a lake freshwater lake in southern Kaftia, it is the largest lake in the [[Burad Lake System]], a series of lakes and rivers that stretch between southern Maianka and western Burawa. The lake is 210.6 km from edge to edge and is composed of two parts, the upper and lower lake that are conjoined at the [[Ufyen Narrows]]. The lake sits mostly within [[Burawa]], excluding it's northeastern side that lies in [[Maianka]]. The lake is fed primarily from the [[Buran River]] via the [[Buran River#Wuga River|Wuga River]] from it's upper lake and then drains towards the it's lower lake towards [[Lake Maianka]] and ultimately the Mesogean.

Lake Haro has been seen as the cradle of the western Kaftian civilization, as a navigable and defensible along it's northern coast and shallow fertile southern coast has made it home to many ancient civilizations. 


== Etymology ==
The name comes from the [[Ufyen Language|Ufyen]] language meaning "Great Sea".

== Geography ==
Lake Haro empties into Lake Maianka via the [[Besor River]] and [[Besor Locks]]. Lake Haro is the largest freshwater lake in Burawa, and third largest in Kaftia. Lake Haro has a surface area of 4,300.29 mi² (11,137.70 km2). It has a maximum length of 210.67 km and an average depth of  32 fathoms (58.5 meters) with a maximum depth of 42 fathoms. The lake is primarily fed by the [[Buran River]] via rainfall from the [[Iraqwi Highlands]] and [[Waqooyiga Valleys]] in the summer seasons.  The lake originally was the final inlet of the Mesogean sea as part of the [[Saga Depression]] before being closed due to receding tides during the last glaciation period.

The upper lake stretches from the mouth of the Wuga River to the Ufyen Narrows, it is a deeper narrower segment of the lake. The city of [[Jowhar]] stretches it's southern coast and is home to the largest inland port in Burawa, the [[Jowhar Port]]. The Upper Lake is only 20% of the surface area of Lake Haro but contains 40% of it's water due to it's depth.

The Lower Lake occupies the 80% of the surface area of Lake Haro with cities such as [[Badalga]], [[Ufyen]], and [[Khar]].

== History ==
Lake Haro was first recorded in during [[Ancient Ufyen|Ufyen antiquity]] where it was given it's name. The area was under the control of multiple factions under the Ufyen Kingfrom until 926, where it was merged into the [[Burawi Kingdom]] during the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa]].

The lake was a highly productive source of reliable nutrient rich freshwater and had been subject to various domestic faction control for ownership.

In 1873 the [[Wuga Lock System|Wuga river lock project]] was constructed to allow ships easier movement between the lake and the Buran river, enabling a rapid development of industry along it's shores.